## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)

1. **Compelling empirical motivation for in-LLM compression over early compression**: Figure 2(a) demonstrates a ~15% performance gap on high-resolution benchmarks when tokens are dropped in early layers vs. keeping all tokens, and Figure 2(b) shows via attention heatmaps across layers 1, 9, 17, 25 that tokens receiving low attention in early layers become critical in later layers (red dotted boxes). This provides concrete evidence that early/Pre-LLM compression irreversibly loses important information, directly motivating the in-LLM approach.

2. **Theoretical proof that K/V compression is a better low-rank approximation than alternatives**: Theorem 3 (Eqs. 12–13) proves that Com(I, C^K, C^V) (the paper's K/V-only compression) approximates full-token attention Com(I, I, I) more closely than Com(C^Q, C^K, C^V) (Pre-LLM/Early-LLM) and Com(C^Q, I, I) (FlexAttention), with probability > 1−o(1). This provides formal justification for the specific design choice of compressing only K/V, as opposed to prior methods that compress all of Q/K/V or only Q.

3. **Strong empirical gains over prior compression methods**: Table 2 shows that ACT-IN-LLM achieves 45.4% average performance on high-resolution benchmarks, a +5.5% improvement over the previous SOTA (FastV at 39.9%), while maintaining 94.0% of the memory footprint of the full-token baseline. Notably, "Ours w/o train" (43.5%) outperforms all trained baselines except FastV, demonstrating plug-and-play effectiveness.

4. **Competitive performance with non-compression models at much lower cost**: Table 3 shows that the 7B-scale model achieves 71.4% average high-resolution performance with ~1K vision tokens and 1.2M SFT data, reaching 87.2% of InternVL2's performance (81.8%) while using only 32.8% of the vision tokens and <24% of the SFT data. This directly validates the efficiency claim from the abstract.

### Supporting strengths

1. **Unified formalization of compression strategies**: Equation 9 and Table 1 provide a clean, unified formulation of different compression methods as Com(C^Q, C^K, C^V) with different compression matrices, along with explicit complexity comparisons. This makes the design space of token compression strategies systematic and comparable.

2. **Empirical validation that vision token attention is low-rank (supporting Theorem 1)**: Figure 5(a) shows the low-rank degree (LDR) of vision tokens is consistently high across layers for both Vicuna-7B and Qwen2-0.5B, while text tokens have much lower LDR. Figure 5(b) confirms Assumption 1 that vision tokens receive ~13× less attention than text tokens. These jointly ground the theoretical analysis.

3. **Hierarchical compression ratios grounded in observed attention sparsity patterns**: Table 4a shows that hierarchical ratios (r_i < r_j < r_p, rows d–i) outperform plain uniform ratios (rows a–c), and that r^hr > r^lr (rows f–h vs. i) performs better, consistent with Figure 5(a)'s finding that high-resolution tokens are more low-rank and Figure 2(b)'s observation that attention becomes sparser in deeper layers.

4. **Scaling behavior verified across model sizes and data sizes**: Figure 7 demonstrates consistent performance gains when scaling from 0.5B to 7B LLMs and from 0.5M to 1.2M SFT data, showing the method's applicability beyond a single model configuration.

### Presentation / practical strengths

1. **Plug-and-play capability without retraining**: Table 2 shows "Ours w/o train" achieves 43.5% on high-resolution benchmarks, surpassing all trained baselines except FastV, making the method immediately applicable to existing models.

2. **Comprehensive ablation study**: Tables 4a/4b/4c and Table 5 systematically ablate compression ratios, compression mechanisms (attention-weight vs. avg-pooling-1D/2D vs. learnable projection), compression layer positions (early/middle/latter/uniform), and the proportion of ACM layers (50%–90%), providing practical guidance for configuration.

## Summary

The paper proposes ACT-IN-LLM, a method that adaptively compresses vision tokens within LLM layers by retaining all query tokens while compressing only key/value tokens in self-attention, guided by text-aware attention weights from the previous layer. The single most important piece of evidence is Table 2, which shows a +5.5% average improvement over the prior SOTA compression method on high-resolution benchmarks (45.4% vs. 39.9%), while Table 3 further demonstrates competitive performance with non-compression models (71.4% vs. InternVL2's 81.8%) using only ~1K vision tokens and <24% of the training data—validating both the effectiveness and efficiency claims.

