This paper extends L₂Boost to interval-censored data by substituting censoring unbiased transformations for the true response. The theoretical results, while sound within their (conditional) assumptions, largely parallel Bühlmann & Yu (2003) with $\hat{Y}_1$ replacing $g(Y)$. The three major weaknesses—incomplete optimality claims that ignore $\hat{S}$ estimation error, experimentally trivial baseline (only midpoint imputation), and 1D-only experiments—collectively prevent the paper from convincingly establishing that it advances either the theoretical or practical state of the art. Papers with similar profiles (restrictive theory + limited baselines) like 5MlPrLO52d scored around 5.5 and were rejected, while papers with more comprehensive validation (ReItdfwMcg, nUBLhhVM1l) scored 6–7. This paper falls below those due to its weaker empirical evidence and the gap in the theoretical claims.

